WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.584
A bearer token grants authority to whoever
possesses

2
00:00:03.584 --> 00:00:06.861
it while it remains acceptable to the
service.

3
00:00:06.861 --> 00:00:11.005
Short lifetime reduces exposure; it does
not make theft harmless.

4
00:00:11.005 --> 00:00:13.511
Use HTTPS and protect stored tokens.

5
00:00:13.511 --> 00:00:17.655
Keep bearer credentials out of URLs, where
browser history, proxies,

6
00:00:17.655 --> 00:00:21.510
analytics, referrer data, and access logs
can retain them.

7
00:00:21.510 --> 00:00:25.558
Choose transport and browser storage for
the application's request model.

8
00:00:25.558 --> 00:00:28.834
Authorization headers allow explicit
attachment to API requests.

9
00:00:28.834 --> 00:00:32.882
JavaScript-readable storage is accessible
to code executing in that origin.

10
00:00:32.882 --> 00:00:36.063
HttpOnly cookies reduce direct script
access, but browsers

11
00:00:36.063 --> 00:00:38.954
attach cookies automatically under their
cookie rules.

12
00:00:38.954 --> 00:00:41.845
Use Secure, appropriate SameSite and scope
attributes,

13
00:00:41.845 --> 00:00:44.736
and CSRF protections suited to the
application.

14
00:00:44.736 --> 00:00:47.049
HttpOnly does not stop malicious in-origin

15
00:00:47.049 --> 00:00:49.170
code from issuing authenticated requests.

16
00:00:49.170 --> 00:00:53.217
No storage choice removes the need to
prevent script injection.

17
00:00:53.217 --> 00:00:57.940
Refresh tokens obtain new access tokens
through the intended refresh flow; they

18
00:00:57.940 --> 00:01:02.373
are not ordinary API access credentials
and need not be JWTs.

19
00:01:02.373 --> 00:01:05.650
They are usually longer-lived and deserve
stronger custody.

20
00:01:05.650 --> 00:01:08.348
Use the identity provider's supported
client or

21
00:01:08.348 --> 00:01:11.529
session binding, rotation, revocation, and
reuse detection.

22
00:01:11.529 --> 00:01:14.709
For OAuth public clients, current security
guidance requires

23
00:01:14.709 --> 00:01:17.986
rotation or sender-constrained refresh
tokens to detect replay.

24
00:01:17.986 --> 00:01:21.648
Keep refresh handling within narrowly
defined endpoints and permissions.

25
00:01:21.648 --> 00:01:23.576
Logout does not automatically invalidate

26
00:01:23.576 --> 00:01:25.696
every issued stateless access token.

27
00:01:25.696 --> 00:01:28.395
Removing the local copy and revoking
refresh

28
00:01:28.395 --> 00:01:31.189
access prevents some future use, while
outstanding

29
00:01:31.189 --> 00:01:34.081
access tokens may remain acceptable until
expiration.

30
00:01:34.081 --> 00:01:36.490
Depending on risk, use session records

31
00:01:36.490 --> 00:01:39.381
or targeted revocation state for earlier
cutoff.

32
00:01:39.381 --> 00:01:42.465
Broad signing-key rollover is an incident
measure only

33
00:01:42.465 --> 00:01:45.646
when verifiers also stop trusting affected
keys; ordinary

34
00:01:45.646 --> 00:01:48.923
rotation with overlap does not revoke them
immediately.

35
00:01:48.923 --> 00:01:52.778
Never log complete JWTs, bearer headers,
or refresh tokens.

36
00:01:52.778 --> 00:01:55.669
Prefer request identifiers, authentication
results, configured issuer

37
00:01:55.669 --> 00:01:58.657
identifiers, and approved key or subject
metadata.

38
00:01:58.657 --> 00:02:01.837
Treat unverified header metadata as
untrusted: bound and

39
00:02:01.837 --> 00:02:05.210
sanitize it, and do not label it
authenticated.

40
00:02:05.210 --> 00:02:08.583
Review tracing, proxy logs, error
collection, and support

41
00:02:08.583 --> 00:02:11.860
exports so they do not silently capture
credentials.

